#: Game/Levels/TutorialWorld.lean:17
msgid "# Tutorial World\n"
"\n"
"## The setup\n"
"\n"
"Welcome! Here you'll prove some geometric statements and pick up your first Lean tricks.\n"
"Your *goal* (what you're trying to prove) shows up after the ⊢ symbol in the top right box.\n"
"How do you get there? With *tactics*. A tactic is just an instruction you give **Lean** to make progress\n"
"on the proof. Sometimes a tactic splits your goal into smaller partial goals. Once you close all of them,\n"
"you'll see 'Level Completed! 🎉' and you can move on.\n"
"\n"
"**Read this:** read the help texts as you go. You'll learn a lot more that way!\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"## The language\n"
"\n"
"In **Lean**, everything (points, lines, sets of points, theorems, proofs) is a *term*, and each term has a *type*,\n"
"exactly one. We write §0 to say that the term §1 has type §2 (read the ':' as 'is an element of'). And since\n"
"everything is a term, *types* are terms too (of type §3, which is a term of type §4, and so on).\n"
"For example, a point §5 is a term of type §6 (our name for the plane). We write §7.\n"
"The plane §8 itself has type §9 (a special type whose terms are things like the plane, or the real numbers).\n"
"\n"
"There's another special type called §10. A term of type §11 is a true-or-false statement, like 'it rains', '2 + 2 = 4'\n"
"or '2 + 3 = 7' (yes, false statements count too). Each statement is itself a type, and its terms\n"
"are *proofs* of it. So a term of type §12 is a proof that 2 + 2 = 4.\n"
"We write §13 to say: §14 is a proof of §15, and we can use it in our proof.\n"
"If we ever got our hands on §16, we could prove anything, like §17 or §18. Fair enough:\n"
"it's like saying 'Give me a lever and a place to stand, and I shall move the world', but in **Lean**."
msgstr ""

#: Game.lean:8
msgid "# Welcome to the Hilbert Geometry game\n"
"#### Learning axiomatic geometry through Lean.\n"
"\n"
"In this game, we'll visit some of the axioms Hilbert proposed to describe\n"
"the plane. By the end, you'll have proved the *Plane Separation Theorem*. Yes, really!\n"
"\n"
"You'll do it by solving little puzzles in a proof assistant called Lean.\n"
"No Lean experience needed. We'll start from zero.\n"
"\n"
"# Read this.\n"
"\n"
"Learning an interactive theorem prover takes a bit of time, so don't rush.\n"
"To get the most out of the game, read the help texts like this one. They're there for you.\n"
"\n"
"Ready? Click on \"Tutorial World\" to start.\n"
"\n"
"## More\n"
"\n"
"Click on the three lines in the top right and select \"Game Info\" for resources,\n"
"links, and ways to chat with the Lean community."
msgstr ""

